# Ch22 Alimentary Tract and Pancreatic Disease

## Anatomy and Physiology

## Ivestigations of GI diseases

- **Principles of Ix of GI disease** - test of structure, test of infection, teset of function

### Imaging

### Endoscopy

- Videoendoscopes provide high-definition imaging and accessories for both diagnostic and therapeutic procedures
- **Imaging modalies in endoscopy** - varying imaging modalities to detect subtle dysplastic changes:
  - Standard 'white light' endoscopy
  - confocal endomicroscopy
  - Autofluorescence
  - Narrow band imaging
- **Therapeutic procedures in endoscopy:**
  - Control of bleeding
  - Endoscopic treatment of tumours
  - Treatment of strictures
  - Management of biliary and pancreatic disease
- **Upper endoscopy** - visualisation of the esophagus, stomach, D1 and D2:
  - <u>Anasthesia</u> - light IV benzodiazepine sedation and local anaesthetic throat spray
  - <u>Fasting</u> - requires fasting for \> 4h
  - <u>Indications</u>:
    - **Clinical** - 1) dyspepsia (\> 55y or w/ alarm symptoms), 2) Dysphagia, 3) Severe N/V, 4) Acute or chronic UGIB, 5) Weight loss, 6) Ix for malabsorption when suspecting coeliac disease
    - **Imaging** - Abnormal CT or barium meal
    - **Therapeutic** - i.e. Tx of bleeding lesions, dilatation of strictures, or placement of percutaneous gastrostomies
  - <u>C/I</u>:
    - **Haemodynamics** - severe shock (?unless massive UGIB)
    - **Cardiorespiratory disease** - recent ACS, arrhythmia, or severe respiratory disease
    - **C-spine** - atlanto-axial subluxation
    - **Possible perforation**
  - <u>Complications</u>:
    - **Complications related to sedation** - cardiorespiratory depression (e.g. MI, respiratory arrest)
    - **Procedure-specific complications** - aspiration pneumonia, perforation

